<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Sangli State</h1><p class="st-meta">1782 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Sangli</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The most important of the Patwardhan Brahmin states of the southern Maratha country, ruled from Sangli on the Krishna river.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patwardhan">Patwardhans</a> were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chitpavan-brahmins">Chitpavan Brahmin</a> generals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwas</a>, rewarded with lands in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> country. In 1782 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chintamanrao-patwardhan">Chintamanrao Patwardhan</a>, known as Appa Saheb, separated from the family seat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miraj">Miraj</a> and made Sangli his own state, building its fort and the Ganpati temple.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a> in 1818 Sangli became a British protected state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-maratha-country">Southern Maratha Country</a>, later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-states-agency">Deccan States Agency</a>, with a 9-gun salute. Its rulers promoted schools and trade, and Sangli grew into a major market for turmeric and other produce. Chintamanrao II introduced representative institutions in the 20th century.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Sangli acceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and was merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bombay-state">Bombay State</a> in 1948; since 1960 it has been part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> as Sangli district.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sangli-state.jpg" alt="Sangli State" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Robert Alfers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sangli_flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1782 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sangli</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Chintamanrao Patwardhan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Marathi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sangli</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1865&amp;focus=sangli-state" aria-label="Sangli State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6709 1551 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sangli State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7029 1807L6991 1757M7029 1807L7364 1601M7029 1807L7364 1602M7029 1807L7018 1386"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6991 1757h0M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M7018 1386h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7029 1807h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7029 1807h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sangli. <a href="/atlas?year=1865&amp;focus=sangli-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
